// The Enochian Alphabet.
//
// From the wing's research file enochian.verified.json, with its fact-check
// corrections applied and every point it left unresolved either left out or
// stated as uncertain: whose hand drew f. 104, the folio of the second version
// in Sloane MS 3189, Mortlake and Ashmole's date, the extent of the backwards
// dictation, whether Sloane MS 3191 writes the Calls in these letters, the
// Equinox issues and the current Unicode status.
//
// The forms are traced from the drawing of 6 May 1583 in Dee's record (Sloane
// MS 3188, f. 104) as reproduced on Wikimedia Commons, checked against the
// version in Sloane MS 3189 and against Casaubon's engraved Holy Table of 1659
// (traced/enochian/glyphs.json, independently checked). Each glyph is the
// traced path data unchanged. No name or value stands beside the forms on
// f. 104: each form is taken as a letter by its place in the order commonly
// given for Dee's table, which no scholarly source to hand confirms, and the
// page says so; the heading of Casaubon's Specimen (1659) is cited where it
// agrees or differs. The register keeps the order of the Latin values; each
// letter's note gives its place on f. 104. No plate is shown: the
// only image of the leaf to hand is a Commons reproduction whose source is not
// stated. No passage of Dee's is quoted: his wording could not be read in an
// edition.
