        drawn: 'Each form is traced as the centre line of the strokes in the reproduction, all twenty-one at one scale, so that they keep the sizes and proportions given them in Dee’s record, which the note above them appears to ask the reader to keep. The hand of f. 104 wrote with a broad pen, thick and thin; the drawing keeps each letter’s strokes, joins, spurs and its one dot, and sets them in the site’s one even round pen, which runs a little past the tapered tips of the ink. The reproduction is a high-contrast black-and-white rendering: where it fills a counter or knots the ink — the curl of Ged, the lower halves of Un and Or — the course is read from the outline and checked against Sloane MS 3189, and Un and Or are drawn with reservation. A smaller second form written above Veh is not drawn; its note describes it.',
        variants: 'Compared form by form with the version at the end of Liber Loagaeth, British Library, Sloane MS 3189 (the Wikimedia Commons reproduction, a greyscale image 1085 pixels wide, whose folio Commons gives as “r. 57”), which writes the same twenty-one forms from left to right, fourteen and seven, with points between them: it agrees form for form, a little heavier and more compact, with the counter of Ged open where f. 104 fills it, the dot of Pal standing clear of the stem, and no second form above Veh. Meric Casaubon’s True & Faithful Relation (London, 1659), read in two Wellcome copies, prints no table of the alphabet; its engraving of the Holy Table carries seventeen of the forms — not Veh, Na, Ger or Pal — re-cut and regularised: Gal as two arcs set back to back, the foot of Or as two bars, Un as a clean zigzag. The heading of its Specimen plate sets three words in these letters beside their readings in roman letters, “Pagesgem” and “Bacap Laſſos”: read from right to left, its forms for B, G, M and P are the forms taken here as Pa, Ged, Tal and Mals, while its C is a K-like form and its A’s are other forms than Veh and Un. The plate does not say which way its words run, and one of them has five signs for the six letters of “Laſſos”, so it corroborates the pairing in part and is not a key. Modern fonts and charts are not used as a base, and no letter is drawn from a Hebrew or Latin letter it is said to resemble.',
