    scriptNote: 'Twenty-one letters, each with a name and a Latin value. The register runs in the order of those values, A to Z — the site’s own arrangement. On f. 104 the forms stand in two lines, read from right to left, and no name or value stands beside them in the reproduction used. Each form is taken as a letter only by its place in the order commonly given for Dee’s table — Pa, Veh, Ged, Gal, Or, Un, Graph, Tal, Gon, Na, Ur, then Mals, Ger, Drux, Pal, Med, Don, Ceph, Van, Fam, Gisg. No scholarly source to hand confirms that order, and one hobby site confirms its first eleven letters. Sloane MS 3189 writes the same sequence of forms from left to right: it confirms the sequence of the forms, not their names. The one witness to hand that sets words in these letters beside their readings in Latin letters, the heading of the Specimen plate in Casaubon’s edition of 1659, agrees for Pa, Ged, Tal and Mals and writes A and C with other forms. Each letter’s note gives its place on f. 104. The names and values are those of Dee’s table as Wikipedia and the dCode letter list report them. Only two names, Ged and Van, begin with the letter they stand for, so the notes point out the names that mislead. Dee gives the letters no numbers; the number values in some modern tables are later additions. The script runs from right to left.',
